-- RiftAcademy Supabase - Migration 019: seed card_keywords (Phase 4 Stage 1)
--
-- RENUMBERED 017 -> 019 when this landed in the repo, 2026-08-09. Core authored
-- this as 017, but two price-ingest migrations claimed 017 and 018 in the hours
-- between authoring and delivery: 20260809000017_price_ingest_run_traceability
-- (PR #201, merged to integration) and 20260809000018_price_ingest_run_lifecycle
-- (PR #205, open at the time). Both were already applied to live, verified
-- against supabase_migrations.schema_migrations, so 017 and 018 were genuinely
-- taken rather than merely reserved. Only this header line changed; the 740
-- insert statements and the guard/assertion blocks are Core's, byte for byte.
--
-- Authority: Ashwin ruling, 2026-08-09 (Core thread), as part of sign-off on
-- Infra's migration-008 bootstrap-procedure proposal (see
-- handoff_infra-to-core_2026-08-09_migration-008-bootstrap-approval).
--
-- What this closes: `card_keywords` (740 rows, 522 cards) was authored live
-- in Supabase during Phase 4 Stage 1 (keyword extraction from
-- `cards.rules_text`, per CR 807.1.b.3/809.1.b.3/814.1.b.3/823.1.c.2 for the
-- bare-value-presumed-1 rule) and has existed ONLY live ever since - no
-- migration or seed file ever inserted it
-- (`grep -l "insert into card_keywords" supabase/migrations/*.sql
-- supabase/seed/*.sql` returns nothing prior to this file). That made it
-- invisible to Infra's proposed bootstrap dump scope (cards/card_printings/
-- card_bans only) - a from-scratch rebuild via dump+restore would silently
-- lose all 740 rows. This migration is the fix: `card_keywords` now has a
-- real, replayable source of truth, and the live table can be dumped too
-- (belt-and-suspenders) or reconstructed from this file alone.
--
-- value_cost jsonb shape: {"energy": N, "power": [{"kind":"domain","domain":
-- "Fury"}]} or {"kind":"any"} entries; {"power": []} means "any energy, no
-- domain pips required"; alternative/OR costs use {"alternatives":
-- [{...},{...}]} - the one live case is ven-074-166's Empower (1 energy OR
-- one Body pip), approved by Ashwin 2026-08-08.
--
-- Replay-safety: `card_keywords.card_code` has an FK to `cards(card_code)`
-- (ON DELETE CASCADE) and `card_keywords.keyword` has an FK to
-- `keywords(keyword)` (ON DELETE RESTRICT, migration 006). Unlike migrations
-- 015/016 (UPDATE statements, which naturally no-op to zero rows against an
-- empty/partial `cards`), a bare INSERT against a not-yet-populated `cards`
-- table would hard-fail the whole migration on FK violation. `keywords` is
-- a small, early-seeded CR vocabulary (25 rows) and is not the at-risk side.
--
-- Per the approved bootstrap procedure, this migration is expected to reach
-- a fresh database via `supabase migration repair --status applied` AFTER a
-- live data dump/restore (which now includes `card_keywords`) - i.e. it is
-- marked applied without actually re-running its body, matching how
-- migration 008 is already handled for `cards`/`card_bans`. The guard below
-- exists for the other paths (a plain `supabase db reset`, or first push to
-- an already-populated live database) so this file is not silently
-- unsafe/unusable outside the bootstrap flow: skip with a notice if `cards`
-- isn't populated yet, run normally if it is.

begin;

do $$
begin
  if not exists (select 1 from cards limit 1) then
    raise notice 'card_keywords seed: cards table is empty, skipping (expected on a from-scratch rebuild prior to data restore - see migration 008''s equivalent guard reasoning)';
    return;
  end if;


  raise notice 'card_keywords seed: inserted % rows', (select count(*) from card_keywords);
end $$;

-- Replay-safety assertion: `card_keywords` had zero rows before this
-- migration (it is the first and only file that ever inserts into it - see
-- the header note). Every insert above runs inside the surrounding
-- transaction (`begin`/`commit`), so the outcome is necessarily all-or-
-- nothing: either the guard skipped everything (table stays at 0, expected
-- pre-data-restore on a from-scratch rebuild) or all 740 inserts succeeded
-- (a partial outcome would have raised an uncaught FK-violation exception
-- and aborted the whole migration before reaching this point - there is no
-- silent partial-success state to check for). This mirrors migrations
-- 015/016's "assert only the invariant that can actually vary" principle,
-- just with a two-valued invariant (0 or 740) instead of a row-by-row one,
-- because INSERT's failure mode (hard error) differs from UPDATE's
-- (silent no-op).
do $$
declare
  kw_rows int;
begin
  select count(*) into kw_rows from card_keywords;

  if kw_rows = 0 then
    raise notice 'card_keywords seed: cards table was not yet populated - skipped as expected on a from-scratch rebuild prior to data restore';
  elsif kw_rows <> 740 then
    raise exception 'card_keywords seed: % rows present, expected exactly 0 (skipped) or 740 (fully seeded) - unexpected partial state', kw_rows;
  end if;
end $$;

commit;
